WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.000
Do you love watching people climb up buildings&nbsp;
and throw themselves off of them in cool ways?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.480
If you do, these lesser-known parkour&nbsp;
action movies are not to be missed.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:14.200
Frenchman David Belle, the founding father of&nbsp;
parkour, brought together a group of like-minded,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:19.360
physically fit people and taught them "la&nbsp;
méthode naturelle" — or natural movement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:23.360
Belle saw parkour as a solid way to train,&nbsp;
but some of his friends thought it needed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:28.200
more flair. This led to the development of&nbsp;
free-running, a parkour offshoot that includes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:33.120
flips and fancy tricks. These differences of&nbsp;
opinion led Belle to split from the group,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:38.360
which would become known as the Yamakasi — a&nbsp;
Congolese word meaning "strong in one's person."

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:44.080
In 1998, the Yamakasi got their big break&nbsp;
after catching the imagination of Luc Besson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:49.360
who saw their high-flying antics on a TV news&nbsp;
report. The French filmmaker cast them as ninjas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:56.480
in the film "Taxi 2," then decided to write a&nbsp;
full screenplay based on the team. "Yamakasi"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:01.640
became the first genuine parkour movie, and its&nbsp;
thrilling action sequences still stand up today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:06.120
The movie follows the team as they attempt to&nbsp;
raise money for a sick boy who needs a new heart.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:10.960
Nine-year-old Jamel suffered a cardiac arrest&nbsp;
after falling from a tree, and a shady surgeon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:15.520
is trying to rip the boy's mother off. When the&nbsp;
Yamakasi find out, they start hitting the houses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:21.080
of the hospital's board of directors, hoping&nbsp;
to put enough cash together to save the kid.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:25.680
Taylor Lautner was a big name in Hollywood when&nbsp;
his New York-set parkour action flick "Tracers"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:31.200
started shooting in 2013. The former Twilight&nbsp;
star plays Cam, a bike messenger in heaps of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:36.120
debt with increasingly impatient gangsters. He's&nbsp;
living out of a rented garage and struggling to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:40.920
keep his head above water when a chance encounter&nbsp;
with high-flying beauty Nikki sends him tumbling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:45.880
headfirst into a world of crime and free-running.&nbsp;
Nikki is part of a group that uses parkour to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:50.543
pull off heists around the city, and Cam has&nbsp;
to learn the ropes fast if he wants to join.

00:01:50.543 --> 00:01:54.823
"That's the great thing about parkour,&nbsp;
my friend. There are no rules."

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:57.720
Obviously, Lautner had to be&nbsp;
in top shape for the role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:01.520
and he ended up doing all his own&nbsp;
stunts. He told Entertainment Weekly —

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:06.520
"It was actually quite surprising to me. I did&nbsp;
extensive training in L.A. with the best people in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:11.880
parkour, but there were some pretty insane stunts,&nbsp;
and I think they just about let me do everything."

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:15.920
Lautner's physicality and commitment made&nbsp;
for some impressive action sequences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:20.880
but the film failed to find an audience. It&nbsp;
didn't come out until 2015, when Twilight-mania&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:25.920
was fading. However, some critics were able to&nbsp;
appreciate "Tracers" for what it is — an often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:30.480
exhilarating love letter to the Big Apple.&nbsp;
The New York Times wrote in its review —

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:36.400
"[The] film shows love not just for stunts,&nbsp;
but for the dynamic surfaces of the city."
 &nbsp;

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:41.840
"The Chronicles of Narnia" star William Moseley&nbsp;
leads the line in 2013's "Run," a film about a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:47.040
teenage parkour enthusiast who gets talked into&nbsp;
becoming a cat burglar by his old man. Petty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:51.720
crook Mike and his son Daniel travel from city&nbsp;
to city hitting jewelry stores and pawn shops.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:55.640
They've been avoiding returning to New York&nbsp;
City on account of Daniel's mob boss uncle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:58.920
who blames Mike for the death of his&nbsp;
sister — but it looks like they've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:02.800
run out of other places to go. When they&nbsp;
return, Daniel quickly falls in with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:07.222
city's free-running crowd — and he starts&nbsp;
to question his allegiance to his father.

00:03:07.222 --> 00:03:13.302
"It's self-expression. Moving through&nbsp;
your environment, freeing your body."

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:18.200
"Run" was a labor of love for writer-director&nbsp;
Simone Bartesaghi, who decided to quit his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:22.480
managing job and begin working on a movie&nbsp;
about parkour after falling in love with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:26.880
it. There was some buzz about the script,&nbsp;
and Bartesaghi won the Milan International&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:31.400
Film Festival's Award for Best Screenplay.&nbsp;
So why hasn't anyone heard of the film?

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:33.360
Critics weren't too fond of the other aspects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:37.800
of "Run" — especially its ending. As&nbsp;
Film Intuition wrote in its review —

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:41.040
"Ignoring the prospect of what might have&nbsp;
been a far better payoff for the subject&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:45.000
matter involving a Parkour contest that’s&nbsp;
mentioned and then immediately discarded,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:48.960
'Run' instead takes a gangster&nbsp;
B-movie shortcut in its final act."

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:52.520
That doesn't mean "Run" isn't worth your&nbsp;
time, though. The review continued —

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:55.480
"Parkour enthusiasts will still&nbsp;
want to give Run a spin since so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:59.680
few representations of what they do have&nbsp;
made their way to the silver screen."

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:05.240
David Belle had parted ways with the Yamakasi by&nbsp;
the time Luc Besson wrote the film of the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:10.160
name, but Besson would go on to work with&nbsp;
parkour's pioneer in a later project. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:16.160
director co-wrote and produced 2004's "District&nbsp;
13," a frenetic French actioner that takes place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:21.480
in an area of Paris that's been overrun by crime.&nbsp;
It's become so bad that Parisian authorities have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:25.480
walled the district off from the rest of the&nbsp;
city, leaving the poor inhabitants to fend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:29.960
for themselves. When a government bomb is&nbsp;
smuggled into the slums, Leito teams up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:35.360
undercover cop Damien to find it — uncovering a&nbsp;
sinister plot by the ruling class in the process.

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:40.400
Helmed by Pierre Morel, who would go on to&nbsp;
direct Liam Neeson in "Taken," "District 13"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:45.120
was well-received in France and the US. While&nbsp;
most acknowledged that the plot wasn't exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:50.440
groundbreaking, the stunning action sequences more&nbsp;
than made up for it. In a glowing review, The New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:55.600
York Times waxed lyrical about the athleticism&nbsp;
and artistry on show throughout, saying —

00:04:55.600 --> 00:05:00.600
"Parkour isn't par-for-the-course movie mayhem,&nbsp;
but a gorgeously choreographed gymnastics of pain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:05.680
that elevates 'District 13' over the impossible&nbsp;
missions and last stands of the season."

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:10.680
The vast majority of critics agree that "District&nbsp;
13" is worth a watch. This white-knuckle roller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:16.800
coaster ride holds an impressive 80 percent&nbsp;
rating on Rotten Tomatoes — certified fresh.

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:21.000
The 2009 sequel to "District 13"&nbsp;
brought gravity-defying stars David&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:25.200
Belle and Cyril Raffaelli back together&nbsp;
for another thrill ride through dystopian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:30.040
Paris. "District 13: Ultimatum" sees our&nbsp;
heroes continue their efforts to bring down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:34.440
the walls surrounding the slums and rid&nbsp;
the area of drug dealers for good. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:38.280
there's mass unrest after two police&nbsp;
officers turn up dead in the district,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:43.120
but Leito and Damien soon discover that it's&nbsp;
a frame job. Corrupt government agents plan to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:48.120
blow up the five tower blocks at the center of the&nbsp;
district and replace them with luxury apartments.

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:52.720
"Ultimatum" isn't too dissimilar to the first&nbsp;
"District 13" in terms of set-up, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:58.040
action is definitely taken up a notch. While the&nbsp;
original concentrated largely on Belle's parkour,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:03.000
Luc Besson wanted to make sure to make the most of&nbsp;
Raffaelli's martial arts skills this time around.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:08.240
Both men pull off multiple physically impressive&nbsp;
feats during the movie's 106-minute runtime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:11.760
which flies by in what feels like&nbsp;
an instant. Like its predecessor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:19.000
"District 13: Ultimatum" is certified fresh&nbsp;
on Rotten Tomatoes, with a 74 percent rating.

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:24.080
Paris is swapped out for Detroit in 2014's "Brick&nbsp;
Mansions," the American remake of "District&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:29.880
13." Luc Besson and David Belle were once again&nbsp;
involved, but Cyril Raffaelli was replaced by Paul&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:34.760
Walker in what would end up being his penultimate&nbsp;
movie appearance. It was a move Belle approved of:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:39.400
when one fan complained about the change on&nbsp;
Twitter, Belle defended the decision, tweeting —

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:42.960
"Personally I like very much the work&nbsp;
Paul Walker has done. The film will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:46.400
be a new experience for me, and I&nbsp;
look forward to working with him."

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:50.600
As is usually the case with Hollywood remakes&nbsp;
of foreign films, "Brick Mansions" didn't live&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:55.280
up to the original. There isn't as much&nbsp;
breathtaking parkour as "District 13" and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:59.520
Paul Walker doesn't match up to Raffaelli&nbsp;
in terms of combat ability — but there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:03.840
an undeniable chemistry between the two&nbsp;
leads. Belle told We Got This Covered —

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:07.880
"When we first met, we sort of looked each&nbsp;
other up and sized each other up. But from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:12.960
the very first shot, the shells broke, and we&nbsp;
realized that we actually really like each other."

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:14.760
"Got a plan?"

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:17.408
"Kick his ass, don't get squashed."

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:21.680
When Daniel Craig's James Bond chases a nimble&nbsp;
terrorist across the rooftops of Madagascar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:26.400
in "Casino Royale," that's no ordinary&nbsp;
stuntman he's pursuing. Bombmaker Mollaka&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:30.960
is played by Sebastien Foucan, an old friend&nbsp;
of David Belle and one of parkour's earliest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:35.280
practitioners. When Belle and Foucan came to&nbsp;
an impasse over the future of their passion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:39.520
the latter moved to the United Kingdom and&nbsp;
started calling the activity "free-running"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:43.040
instead. It was in northern England that&nbsp;
the Frenchman got the chance to showcase his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:46.800
vision for free-running by co-starring&nbsp;
in the action film "The Tournament."

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:51.240
In writer-director Scott Mann's universe, a&nbsp;
society of rich folks gather the best assassins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:56.520
in the world for a big shoot-out every seven&nbsp;
years. The killers all descend on a single town,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.520 --> 00:08:00.680
and the last one standing walks away&nbsp;
with a cool $10 million. Ving Rhames,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:05.280
Robert Carlyle, and Liam Cunningham all&nbsp;
feature — but when it comes to the action,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:09.840
it's Foucan who truly impresses. The free-runner's&nbsp;
flowing moves work perfectly with the breakneck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:13.400
pace of the movie, often distracting&nbsp;
viewers from some of its unfortunate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:17.880
narrative shortcomings. As Rob Hunter said&nbsp;
in his review for Film School Rejects —

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:21.480
"Sebastien Foucan's leap onto&nbsp;
a moving bus is pretty sweet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:24.840
and while I don't pretend to understand&nbsp;
the motivation behind some of his more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:29.240
extraneous parkour moves, I have to&nbsp;
admit I'm entertained watching them."

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:35.280
"Freerunner" was made on a much lower budget than&nbsp;
most action films, and it definitely shows. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:39.680
there's no denying that the parkour on display&nbsp;
is outstanding. Lawrence Silverstein's film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:44.400
follows Ryan as he races across Cleveland with&nbsp;
an explosive collar strapped around his neck.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:48.600
He's one of eight free-runners selected to&nbsp;
take part in a contest in which the winner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:53.920
gets $1 million and the losers die. As soon&nbsp;
as one participant crosses the finish line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:58.560
the remaining collars detonate. Shady&nbsp;
businessmen watch the action unfold from afar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:04.480
placing bets on the outcome. Ridiculous? Maybe.&nbsp;
Worth watching? If you love inventive parkour&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:08.820
and crazy stunts, absolutely. Movie&nbsp;
Mavericks declared in its review —

00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:11.440
"'Freerunner' is 'Fast and&nbsp;
Furious' without the cars."

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:15.560
International Freerunning Champion Ryan&nbsp;
Doyle appeared in the film and was also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:20.240
in charge of choreography, but some of his&nbsp;
work was sadly undone in post-production.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:24.480
Some shots in "Freerunner" seem like they&nbsp;
end far too early — but the movie is worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:28.400
checking out even just for snippets&nbsp;
of the incredibly impressive moves.

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:32.480
"All I know is, after the race&nbsp;
today, something just clicked."

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:33.720
"Yeah, it's called a concussion."

00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:35.440
"No, it's a revelation."

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:39.800
"Ong-Bak: The Thai Warrior" introduced&nbsp;
Tony Jaa to the wider world in spectacular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.800 --> 00:09:44.400
fashion. Mixing the intensity of Bruce&nbsp;
Lee with the acrobatics of Jackie Chan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:49.280
Jaa is mesmerizing as humble muay thai&nbsp;
master Ting, who lives in a rural village in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.280 --> 00:09:54.280
Thailand. When thugs from Bangkok decapitate the&nbsp;
village's prized Buddha statue and steal its head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.280 --> 00:09:58.480
Ting decides to follow them — and when he&nbsp;
discovers that the mob has it in their possession,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:03.120
he throws himself head first into the underworld.&nbsp;
The fight scenes in "Ong-Bak" are gritty and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:07.880
impeccably choreographed, but watching Jaa&nbsp;
run away from fights is just as entertaining.

00:10:07.880 --> 00:10:12.120
In one of the movie's most famous scenes,&nbsp;
Ting goes into full free-runner mode as he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:16.560
chased through the narrow lanes of central&nbsp;
Bangkok by over a dozen angry henchmen. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:20.720
six-minute sequence goes from impressive to&nbsp;
off the charts as the out-of-towner performs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.720 --> 00:10:24.480
front flips through narrow panes of glass&nbsp;
and cannonballs through a ring of barbed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:29.760
wire that no man his size has any right fitting&nbsp;
through. Jaa turns his pursuers into a human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:33.880
parkour course when cornered, using them&nbsp;
as stepping stones before scaling a wall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:39.329
and escaping. The critics absolutely loved it&nbsp;
— and if you're an action fan, you will too.

